["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Thinned crust should sink, yet the American West stands a mile high The evidence for plate stretching is usually buried under sediment. The Basin and Range Province in the United States is the rare exception: the crust has already been pulled to roughly twice its width and is still extending, and GPS, the paleoseismic record and detachment faults let us see it happening. Geology Bites Geology Bites Brian Wernicke on Lithospheric Extension and the Basin and Range Province Structural geology Basin and Range Lithospheric extension Detachment faults GPS crustal deformation Basin and Range Province lithospheric extension low-angle detachment faults dismembered thrust sheets\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-geologybites-地壳拉伸应沉陷-盆岭省却仍高一英里/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_episode/1251669/1251669-1787864599777-55cade44ca8de.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">34:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Geology Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Thinned crust should sink, yet the American West stands a mile high</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The evidence for plate stretching is usually buried under sediment. The Basin and Range Province in the United States is the rare exception: the crust has already been pulled to roughly twice its width and is still extending, and GPS, the paleoseismic record and detachment faults let us see it happening.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Structural geology</span><span class=\"tag\">Basin and Range</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Buys Industry-Wide Default Limits The headline number is the least interesting part: what Meta actually bought is two-hour time locks, overnight shutoffs and age assurance turned into defaults — plus the $18 billion-versus-$12 billion gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join. The Journal The Journal Will Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Change Social Media? Meta TikTok Minor protection Regulatory settlement Default settings Age assurance COPPA age assurance algorithmic recommendations\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-wsjournal-meta-180亿和解-买的是行业默认限制/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/b84f9932-8174-11e9-bbd8-7f261513f0c9/image/d84a4f4d55158df9d14f6f2d4b219a28.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">22:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Journal</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Buys Industry-Wide Default Limits</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The headline number is the least interesting part: what Meta actually bought is two-hour time locks, overnight shutoffs and age assurance turned into defaults — plus the $18 billion-versus-$12 billion gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Meta</span><span class=\"tag\">TikTok</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Before AI slips control, Plan A is the only brake that arrives in time Plan A uses transparent research, capped compute and a citizens&#x27; dividend to pull AI off an exponential explosion and onto a governable slope; its author still puts the odds of catastrophe at 15%, but rates that better than sitting and waiting for AI takeoff. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours AI 2027&#x27;s author returns with a plan to change the ending | Daniel Kokotajlo AI governance Superintelligence AGI timelines Plan A Alignment problem Compute controls Plan A horizon-length neuralese superpersuasion privacy-preserving auditing mutual assured compute destruction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eightythousand-plan-a-和-ai-赛跑的最不坏方案/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/l3bOMZM_lVkHMg6r7-Y159Jbw3z8SopNbk7ZvNOGS3Q/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS9hNTk5/NDgzNjI5NjViNTI1/ZjE2MzBmZTdkZjFi/MjcyMC5qcGc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">3:47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Before AI slips control, Plan A is the only brake that arrives in time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plan A uses transparent research, capped compute and a citizens&#x27; dividend to pull AI off an exponential explosion and onto a governable slope; its author still puts the odds of catastrophe at 15%, but rates that better than sitting and waiting for AI takeoff.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI takeover isn&#x27;t a 2029 problem, it&#x27;s a 2028 problem Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated somewhere between 2028 and 2031, at which point progress runs 4-5x faster per year and takeover risk is extremely high; his proposal for slowing down is a US-China compute transparency treaty. The MAD Podcast MAD Podcast AI Could Take Over in 2029. Is It Already Too Late? | Ryan Greenblatt AI risk Superintelligence timelines Compute governance Valuation impact GDP growth Zuckerberg RSI / recursive self-improvement Reward hacking Hill climbing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-madpod-ai-全面接管或发生在-2029-治理窗口只剩三年/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_nologo/40657026/40657026-1768486187830-f8f544fbc7c46.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The MAD Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI takeover isn&#x27;t a 2029 problem, it&#x27;s a 2028 problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated somewhere between 2028 and 2031, at which point progress runs 4-5x faster per year and takeover risk is extremely high; his proposal for slowing down is a US-China compute transparency treaty.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI risk</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence timelines</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"After GLP-1: Injection Is No Longer the Barrier, and Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era Injectables are no longer a dealbreaker, and peptide drugs are extending from weight loss into exercise mimetics and neural modulation. The scientist&#x27;s caveat: outside GLP, the data is still thin, and the approval cycle can run 20 years. From Our Neurons to Yours From Our Neurons to Yours After Ozempic: the revolution in peptide science | Jonathan Long Peptide drugs GLP-1 Exercise mimetics Weight loss Drug development Biotech GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) Lac-Phe (lactoyl-phenylalanine) Unimolecular polyagonist AGRP neurons (agouti-related peptide neurons) POMC neurons (pro-opiomelanocortin neurons) MOTRPAC (Molecular Transducers of Physical Activity Consortium)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-fromourneurons-glp-1之后-肽科学正在开启医学新大门/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/vqsbkvixwt8cyaq4nqd5od1ubzc3?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">49:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From Our Neurons to Yours</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>After GLP-1: Injection Is No Longer the Barrier, and Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Injectables are no longer a dealbreaker, and peptide drugs are extending from weight loss into exercise mimetics and neural modulation. The scientist&#x27;s caveat: outside GLP, the data is still thin, and the approval cycle can run 20 years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Peptide drugs</span><span class=\"tag\">GLP-1</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"High long-end yields aren&#x27;t policy weakness — AI data centers are bidding away the capital At Jackson Hole, Kansas City Fed President Schmid said the 30-year Treasury climbing above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for capital; his personal judgment is that monetary conditions are still on the loose side, and inflation running up into the mid-3s has to be dealt with. Odd Lots Odd Lots Kansas City Fed President Jeffrey Schmid on the First Jackson Hole of the Warsh Era Monetary policy Inflation Long-end rates Federal Reserve AI infrastructure Payments innovation atomic settlement r-star reaction function forward guidance dual mandate dissent\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-oddlots-长端利率高企不是政策软弱-是实体需求在抢资金/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/playlist/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/8a94442e-5a74-4fa2-8b8d-ae27003a8d6b/982f5071-765c-403d-969d-ae27003a8d83/image.jpg?t=1681322812&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">28:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>High long-end yields aren&#x27;t policy weakness — AI data centers are bidding away the capital</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">At Jackson Hole, Kansas City Fed President Schmid said the 30-year Treasury climbing above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for capital; his personal judgment is that monetary conditions are still on the loose side, and inflation running up into the mid-3s has to be dealt with.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Monetary policy</span><span class=\"tag\">Inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding Startup strategy Enterprise sales Talent acquisition Product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早卖企业-用界面和速度赢下巨头/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/0d97354a-306b-45f5-bf26-a8d81eef47ec/ed2664df-9371-438e-8baf-dd2ee0fdde87/3000x3000/thea16zshow-podcastcoverart-3000x3000.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-数据是-ai-时代唯一护城河-合法权限代理最危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/dc36d7b4-a1cb-11f1-a66e-afd86ce968a2/image/14007c74e6b09456b85f85da756966ab.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive investing Index funds Trading costs Investment philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指数基金为收盘价多付4-被动投资不只是指数/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i1.ytimg.com/vi/Ly4DVGaoqy4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro policy Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通胀预期失锚-美联储最怕的不是关税/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://content.production.cdn.art19.com/images/71/5b/8e/71/715b8e71-0bdf-42d3-bd31-bd55674c74b8/b6a1944de87c528e7b186c22cb1583d6816630a375675e194f6abe4b98ac796808b5b96d4827d496c2bba16d5a00895be2616c9ef2de840178324bbbdd2c658e.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? Sunscreen Vitamin D Melanoma Chemical filters Evidence-based medicine Skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防晒霜不是毒药-但深肤色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/f521b120-2c30-11e6-b5f3-63ee984ee1a4/image/e7470287efbfa0aff068175bb6c31805.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">Vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolutions Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-没有板块构造就没有生命-地球是幸运的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0m1q0p7.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer Ketogenic diet Mental health Mitochondria Low carb Clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮饮食治疗难治性精神疾病-线粒体是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/fc952864-a1b5-11f1-b8de-73cba0073142/image/c939ff80ce31857dd7c3855498b342f4.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">Mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms Cobalt Congo Supply chain Colonial history Electric vehicles Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-没有刚果的钴-就没有你的智能手机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2493x2493+0+0/resize/3000/quality/66/format/jpg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2F03%2Fc459c6634182aa03e1cb900e6c09%2Fc2f9f79e-2055-4cee-951a-f14cf292e792.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services programming careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/iVW3GwksRPVrFAvHlaUrGM8A5rlNpxkuuBcn_Vxyi10/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS8yZjc0/ODhmYWFlMDIxMzY2/NzgwOTE3MzIyN2Q0/OGQyYy5wbmc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open source EU AI Act AI governance Open source ethics European tech sovereignty Accountability chain zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_episode/44116043/44116043-1785919863707-61ac4e522530b.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science History of Science India Big Science Nuclear Physics Cyclotron State and Science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/vf7zd334cr5yuopglmx6ods7mnyj?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of Science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) RCE (Remote Code Execution) TOCTOU (Time Of Check Time Of Use) WebDAV IMDS (Instance Metadata Service)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-协作失控-安全评估意外攻破内外网/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/95599230-b558-11ee-b177-1b0512fd2559/image/701fb3a802c0fe1fb272911aa040ffc2.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient History Etymology Religious Conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十战九败的汪达尔人-靠断供税收终结西罗马/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f9e74b795a6299fd3b58/1787668990067-945251b4-f431-4532-8d17-e8a45d73909d.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air war Tech iteration Fighter aces Military history Psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一战空战浪漫背后-新飞行员平均活两周/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f67d4b795a40fffd3b2c/1787653671356-be63eb97-fe16-443b-8439-2ced3b8d947e.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air war</span><span class=\"tag\">Tech iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) The Troubles Political History Military Failure IRA British Politics Sectarian Conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英国当年几乎随时接受类似-贝尔法斯特协议-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/00c0a118-2426-11ee-b258-73d331d0123b/image/db3295fcd9d3a7113e8000e931f0541d.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">Political History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI mathematics cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-证明封死-cvp-近似困难-gap-但不烧密码学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/j0mg64drknc1b0xl8t4m5yn6umlo?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI coding Agents Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序员的价值从写码转向调度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://lexfridman.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/powerpress/artwork_3000-230.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">Agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Why performant code matters (but gets widely ignored), with Casey Muratori Performance Architecture Decisions Theoretical Peak AI Coding Compiler Optimization theoretical peak serial dependency chain hotspot branch prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-pragmatic-性能优化不靠剖析热点-靠理论峰值反推差距/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/feed/podcast/458709/post/212722114/3d2ea6282c10c64c309a5052ce60d5c5.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Performance</span><span class=\"tag\">Architecture Decisions</span>\n</div></div></a>"]